    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2869.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 704,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical States (The Three Phases)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we drink, swim in, and what fills our oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as nature’s transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (in our blood, through plant roots, etc.).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When water freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less dense*, which is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how water travels up from a plant's roots to its leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite this abundance, about **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** (found in oceans). Of the remaining fresh water, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. **Less than 1%** of Earth's water is easily accessible fresh water for human use (in lakes, rivers, and accessible aquifers).\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiology and water are inseparable:\n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**.\n*   Water regulates human body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs and tissues.\n*   Scientists searching for life on other planets always start with one simple rule: **\"Follow the water.\"** Where there is liquid water, there is a strong possibility of life.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives our weather and climate, and is the literal lifeblood of every living organism on the planet."
